Therapist: Good to see you, Sarah. How was your week?
Client: Honestly, up and down. Work at Lindqvist & Cole has been a nightmare this month — end of quarter close.
Therapist: What's made it especially hard this time?
Client: My manager keeps piling more onto my plate and I don't feel like I can say no. I've been getting maybe five hours of sleep a night.
Therapist: That's a significant drop from where we started. How has that been affecting you day to day?
Client: I'm just tired all the time, and a little more irritable with my roommate than I'd like to be.
Therapist: I notice you're tearing up right now, talking about this.
Client: Sorry — yeah. It's just a lot. My birthday's actually this month, March 14th, and I always feel like I should have things more figured out by now.
Therapist: That's a really understandable feeling, and it makes sense that a birthday can bring that kind of reflection up alongside everything else that's piling on. Let's try something concrete. I want to walk through the "circle of control" exercise with you — separating out what's actually within your power to change about this workload versus what isn't.
Client: Okay, yeah. I think that could help, because right now everything just feels like my problem to fix.
Therapist: Right, so let's start with the list. What's something about the end-of-quarter crunch that genuinely is within your control?
Client: I guess... whether I say yes to extra projects, or push back and say I'm at capacity.
Therapist: Good. And what's outside your control?
Client: The actual deadline the finance team sets. That's corporate, not my manager.
Therapist: Exactly. Separating those two categories out loud, does that shift anything for you right now?
Client: A little. It's calming, actually. I think I've been treating the whole situation as one big thing I have to solve, instead of picking it apart like that.
Therapist: That's a great insight to end on. For this week, I'd like you to keep a short list — anytime you feel that overwhelmed, jot down one thing in each column, in control or not.
Client: I can do that. I'll use the notes app on my phone.
Therapist: Perfect. Same time next week?
Client: Yes, that works for me. Thanks for today.
